# MAAGAP ML service — environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to ml-service/.env and fill in the values. That file is
# gitignored (.gitignore line 9) and MUST stay that way: the service-role key
# below bypasses every Row Level Security policy in the database.
#
#   cp ml-service/.env.example ml-service/.env
#
# main.py loads ml-service/.env at startup, but never overrides a variable
# that is already exported — an explicit export, a CI secret, or a container's
# own environment always wins over this file.
#
# WHY THESE MATTER: without them the service still starts, still scores, and
# every write-back it performs silently does nothing. Live risk-tier updates
# never reach projects.risk_tier, and monitoring reports sit at "Awaiting
# re-score" forever with a Retry button that cannot resolve them. The service
# warns at startup if they are missing.

# Supabase project URL. NOTE THE NAME: the frontend calls this
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL in frontend/.env.local — the same value, a
# different variable name. Setting only two of the three because of this
# rename is the most common way this ends up half-configured.
SUPABASE_URL=

# Service-role key (frontend/.env.local calls it the same thing). Bypasses
# RLS entirely — server-side only, never expose it to a browser.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Security --------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. Shared secret for the guarded routes — /webhooks/monitoring-report,
# /api/v1/update-monitoring (mutates risk tiers) and /api/v1/run-optimizer
# (spends minutes of CPU), plus the authenticated reads.
#
# The service REFUSES TO START without it. An absent secret must never mean
# "authentication off", which is exactly what it used to mean (S1).
#
# Generate one — placeholder values like "changeme" are rejected by name:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
#
# This value must be IDENTICAL to ML_SERVICE_WEBHOOK_SECRET in the frontend's
# environment; the frontend attaches it as the X-Webhook-Secret header on every
# server-side call (frontend/src/lib/ml-service.ts).
#
# Declared ONCE, deliberately: this file previously listed it twice. Because
# common/settings.py's loader never overwrites a key it has already set, the
# first line won — so filling in the second and leaving the first blank set the
# secret to empty and produced a refusal to start that named a variable you
# had, in fact, filled in.
ML_SERVICE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Local development escape hatch. Set to 1 ONLY to run without a secret on
# localhost; every request then logs a warning. Never set this anywhere
# reachable from outside your machine.
# ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED=0

# Browser origins permitted to call this service directly. Empty by default and
# usually correct: the Next.js frontend calls from the server side, where CORS
# does not apply. Comma-separated.
# ML_SERVICE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://your-app.vercel.app

# Per-client requests per minute on the expensive POST routes. 0 disables.
# ML_SERVICE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=10
